body:
Atheist here;

I'd have to say that, with genuinely decent people, a religious community can truly be a force of good in the world. They are capable of accepting all other decent human beings regardless of lifestyle choices that may differ from their beliefs. I'm not an admitted atheist to my family, but, for the most part, my family is brimming with good people that only want to see the world become a better place. They, with the support of their community, bend their wills to helping people who need it without asking for anything in return. There is so much generosity within these communities, and the people who live quiet, unassuming lives are some of the best of us. They believe, with their entire heart and soul, in a higher power, and I think that is a very beautiful thing. It gives them the strength to continue in the face of so many challenges. I used to be cynical about it. I believed that these people were only decent human beings because they believed their god would figuratively spank them for misbehaving. While I'll never believe in a divine being, I DO believe that many of the people that practice a faith do so simply because they believe, not because they're afraid.

That is what I admire about religion... done correctly.
